WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.090 --> 00:00:03.010
- So it's come to my attention
that Ned from The Try Guys

00:00:03.010 --> 00:00:04.700
has a lot of problems with his knee.

00:00:04.700 --> 00:00:06.360
Being an osteopathic physician,

00:00:06.360 --> 00:00:08.510
one who has some extra
training in sports medicine,

00:00:08.510 --> 00:00:09.840
I'm excited to watch this video.

00:00:09.840 --> 00:00:10.673
Let's get into it.

00:00:10.673 --> 00:00:11.506
Bee-whoop!

00:00:11.506 --> 00:00:14.200
- The fall of 2012, I broke my knee so bad

00:00:14.200 --> 00:00:15.790
that it was cut down to the bone.

00:00:15.790 --> 00:00:17.707
And my friend said, "This is the day

00:00:17.707 --> 00:00:19.280
"that we saw Ned's kneecap"

00:00:19.280 --> 00:00:20.113
- What was cut down?

00:00:20.113 --> 00:00:21.900
I wanna know was his skin like open?

00:00:21.900 --> 00:00:24.390
When people say break knee, I
don't know if it's a fracture

00:00:24.390 --> 00:00:26.810
of their femur, their
tibia, their patella;

00:00:26.810 --> 00:00:27.970
which is just the kneecap.

00:00:27.970 --> 00:00:29.960
- It was playing paintball in the woods

00:00:29.960 --> 00:00:31.230
in rural New Hampshire.

00:00:31.230 --> 00:00:33.450
It's something called a septic knee.

00:00:33.450 --> 00:00:36.820
I cut my knee down to the
bone and then it got infected.

00:00:36.820 --> 00:00:39.780
- Oh okay, so what
happens in a septic joint,

00:00:39.780 --> 00:00:42.550
you get this bacterial infection
within the joint space.

00:00:42.550 --> 00:00:43.760
The knee starts swelling,

00:00:43.760 --> 00:00:45.670
it gets real hot, it gets real red.

00:00:45.670 --> 00:00:47.820
What we do in order to
diagnose it actually,

00:00:47.820 --> 00:00:49.700
outside of seeing that it looks infectious

00:00:49.700 --> 00:00:52.480
is actually put in a
needle, draw fluid out

00:00:52.480 --> 00:00:55.250
and send it for testing
to see the cell counts,

00:00:55.250 --> 00:00:56.720
to see if there's white
blood cells present,

00:00:56.720 --> 00:00:57.980
which can be a sign of an infection.

00:00:57.980 --> 00:01:00.260
We also send a culture of that fluid,

00:01:00.260 --> 00:01:03.130
where we actually try and grow
and cultivate the bacteria

00:01:03.130 --> 00:01:05.040
to see what type of bacteria is present

00:01:05.040 --> 00:01:08.670
and which antibiotics it
could be resistant to.

00:01:08.670 --> 00:01:10.590
- I had multiple surgeries to clean out

00:01:10.590 --> 00:01:13.650
and my knee was completely destroyed.

00:01:13.650 --> 00:01:16.640
I was off work for months
playing video games all day.

00:01:16.640 --> 00:01:20.520
It got to the point where I just...

00:01:20.520 --> 00:01:24.470
Taking painkillers was like
the best part of my day.

00:01:24.470 --> 00:01:28.330
And it was a really, really scary time.

00:01:28.330 --> 00:01:30.620
- Yeah, it's really scary
because treatment options

00:01:30.620 --> 00:01:32.270
for painkillers are quite limited.

00:01:32.270 --> 00:01:34.080
You'd think about over
the counter options,

00:01:34.080 --> 00:01:36.070
you have your nonsteroidal
anti-inflammatories

00:01:36.070 --> 00:01:37.590
like ibuprofen, Motrin.

00:01:37.590 --> 00:01:38.990
Then you have your acetaminophen

00:01:38.990 --> 00:01:40.790
which is like your Tylenols of the world.

00:01:40.790 --> 00:01:43.180
Then after that, fairly limited.

00:01:43.180 --> 00:01:45.550
The next line is really opioid therapy.

00:01:45.550 --> 00:01:46.470
If you have nerve pain

00:01:46.470 --> 00:01:49.150
there's some other options
like gabapentin, Neurontin.

00:01:49.150 --> 00:01:51.330
And then we have like
superficial treatment options

00:01:51.330 --> 00:01:54.650
where you have nonsteroidal
anti-inflammatory, NSAID gel

00:01:54.650 --> 00:01:56.380
like Voltaren Gel diclofenac.

00:01:56.380 --> 00:01:58.580
You also have lidocaine patches,

00:01:58.580 --> 00:02:01.050
which can help numb the area temporarily

00:02:01.050 --> 00:02:01.883
to give you healing.

00:02:01.883 --> 00:02:05.490
But long term, we don't have
great ways of pain control

00:02:05.490 --> 00:02:08.570
except allowing your body
a chance to heal itself.

00:02:08.570 --> 00:02:12.270
- Oxycodone is an opioid drug

00:02:12.270 --> 00:02:15.360
and that's one of the main
factors that's contributing

00:02:15.360 --> 00:02:16.910
to the current opioid crisis.

00:02:16.910 --> 00:02:20.640
- So before we were definitely
overprescribing oxycodone

00:02:20.640 --> 00:02:22.590
and this was like kind of happening

00:02:22.590 --> 00:02:23.820
during my medical training

00:02:23.820 --> 00:02:26.410
where we started to realize
that we're over treating pain.

00:02:26.410 --> 00:02:27.440
This initially started

00:02:27.440 --> 00:02:29.720
obviously with the pharmaceutical
companies playing games

00:02:29.720 --> 00:02:31.600
and misleading doctors
about whether or not

00:02:31.600 --> 00:02:33.130
these medications were addicting,

00:02:33.130 --> 00:02:35.540
but also because of some of the surveys

00:02:35.540 --> 00:02:37.030
that we were giving patients.

00:02:37.030 --> 00:02:38.630
Ask them questions of whether or not

00:02:38.630 --> 00:02:40.300
their pain was treated adequately.

00:02:40.300 --> 00:02:42.980
And doctors were seeing
reimbursements fall meaning

00:02:42.980 --> 00:02:44.430
hospitals weren't getting paid,

00:02:44.430 --> 00:02:46.390
doctors weren't being
reimbursed for their care

00:02:46.390 --> 00:02:49.130
because patients were saying,
I still had a level of pain.

00:02:49.130 --> 00:02:51.690
And as a result, doctors
started over treating pain

00:02:51.690 --> 00:02:53.830
in order to get better
patient satisfaction scores.

00:02:53.830 --> 00:02:56.330
By giving the patients
what they want, fully,

00:02:56.330 --> 00:02:57.700
a hundred percent full stop,

00:02:57.700 --> 00:02:59.966
may not actually be
ideal for their health.

00:02:59.966 --> 00:03:03.610
- A typical situation could
be like, what happened to me.

00:03:03.610 --> 00:03:06.500
You have a horrible
injury, you're off work,

00:03:06.500 --> 00:03:08.820
start taking pain medications

00:03:08.820 --> 00:03:11.080
because you're actually in pain,

00:03:11.080 --> 00:03:13.210
but then you get addicted to it.

00:03:13.210 --> 00:03:15.980
So you continue taking pain medications.

00:03:15.980 --> 00:03:18.510
This isn't about the opioid
crisis in the United States.

00:03:18.510 --> 00:03:19.343
This is about me.

00:03:19.343 --> 00:03:22.510
Most people stopped taking them
within six weeks of surgery

00:03:22.510 --> 00:03:25.300
but here I am three months later

00:03:25.300 --> 00:03:29.810
still taking high doses of
oxycodone every single day.

00:03:29.810 --> 00:03:31.320
- So at some point you need to figure out

00:03:31.320 --> 00:03:33.280
a step down approach or figure out

00:03:33.280 --> 00:03:34.450
why the pain is continuing.

00:03:34.450 --> 00:03:36.690
Is there something that
surgically needs to be addressed?

00:03:36.690 --> 00:03:39.700
Is it about beginning some
sort of rehab protocol?

00:03:39.700 --> 00:03:41.010
And a very interesting one

00:03:41.010 --> 00:03:42.430
that a lot of people don't talk about,

00:03:42.430 --> 00:03:46.883
taking off opioids can
actually reduce pain.

00:03:47.750 --> 00:03:48.757
Kind of an interesting concept.

00:03:48.757 --> 00:03:50.620
It's paradoxical in nature.

00:03:50.620 --> 00:03:53.226
- Over the last six years, I've
still had lingering effects.

00:03:53.226 --> 00:03:55.460
(groaning)
- You good there?

00:03:55.460 --> 00:03:58.520
- Anytime I would bend
over, my knee would pop

00:03:58.520 --> 00:03:59.353
and it would be painful.

00:03:59.353 --> 00:04:03.530
Here I am after soccer icing my knee

00:04:03.530 --> 00:04:04.730
and it makes me think

00:04:04.730 --> 00:04:07.840
that I'm not gonna be able
to jump around and play

00:04:07.840 --> 00:04:08.860
with my kids.

00:04:08.860 --> 00:04:13.050
Is it worth it to willingly
go back and get a surgery

00:04:14.050 --> 00:04:15.440
just for the potential,

00:04:15.440 --> 00:04:17.850
just the possibility of having no pain?

00:04:17.850 --> 00:04:19.180
- So there's a lot of options here.

00:04:19.180 --> 00:04:22.090
There's injectable
options, surgical options.

00:04:22.090 --> 00:04:24.050
I know a lot of people
in the medical community

00:04:24.050 --> 00:04:26.640
like to poopoo acupuncture
and integrative medicine.

00:04:26.640 --> 00:04:28.360
I think that there is
definitely some value there.

00:04:28.360 --> 00:04:30.140
I've actually had good outcomes with it.

00:04:30.140 --> 00:04:33.660
For a patient who's having
knee pain of his age,

00:04:33.660 --> 00:04:35.430
it's reasonable to say,

00:04:35.430 --> 00:04:37.920
maybe it's time that you
don't play soccer anymore.

00:04:37.920 --> 00:04:40.360
Because there's patients of
mine who are 40 years old

00:04:40.360 --> 00:04:42.560
have had knee problems,
knee surgeries in the past

00:04:42.560 --> 00:04:44.970
and are playing pickup
basketball with 20 year olds

00:04:44.970 --> 00:04:46.730
and are constantly having knee pain.

00:04:46.730 --> 00:04:47.940
There's times where in life

00:04:47.940 --> 00:04:50.310
we have to admit that maybe
we can't do that anymore.

00:04:50.310 --> 00:04:51.930
And it doesn't mean that life is terrible,

00:04:51.930 --> 00:04:54.130
it means that adaptations have to be made.

00:04:54.130 --> 00:04:55.720
Some patients will say, I refuse

00:04:55.720 --> 00:04:56.820
for that to be that adaptation.

00:04:56.820 --> 00:04:58.440
I'd rather try the surgery and risk it.

00:04:58.440 --> 00:04:59.570
And that's totally fine

00:04:59.570 --> 00:05:01.093
as long as they're aware of those risks.

00:05:01.093 --> 00:05:03.760
(upbeat music)

00:05:05.930 --> 00:05:07.760
- [Woman] He said that
everything went really well.

00:05:07.760 --> 00:05:09.470
They cleaned out all the scar tissue.

00:05:09.470 --> 00:05:12.650
He also found a minor
meniscus tear, which he fixed.

00:05:12.650 --> 00:05:14.360
- Meniscus, common word used,

00:05:14.360 --> 00:05:15.860
a lot of patients don't know what it is.

00:05:15.860 --> 00:05:18.350
You have a big femur bone
at the top of your thigh,

00:05:18.350 --> 00:05:20.610
a slightly smaller bone
at the tibia in your shin.

00:05:20.610 --> 00:05:22.240
They sit on top of one another.

00:05:22.240 --> 00:05:23.440
In order for your knee to move,

00:05:23.440 --> 00:05:24.670
you need to have good mobility.

00:05:24.670 --> 00:05:28.160
So you have these two C
shaped pads called a meniscus.

00:05:28.160 --> 00:05:29.860
A lateral meniscus and a medial meniscus.

00:05:29.860 --> 00:05:31.440
One's on the outside, one's on the inside.

00:05:31.440 --> 00:05:33.180
And they essentially provide padding,

00:05:33.180 --> 00:05:34.700
extra stability to the knee.

00:05:34.700 --> 00:05:36.470
They actually increase
surface area of the knee

00:05:36.470 --> 00:05:38.410
so you don't have one point of pressure

00:05:38.410 --> 00:05:40.210
of the two bones rubbing on each other.

00:05:40.210 --> 00:05:42.660
- I look down and my knee is swollen up

00:05:42.660 --> 00:05:44.291
the size of a cantaloupe.

00:05:44.291 --> 00:05:46.882
My thing is green, my leg is green.

00:05:46.882 --> 00:05:47.715
So here's the normal leg,
here's the green one.

00:05:48.950 --> 00:05:50.130
- Okay, that's not good.

00:05:50.130 --> 00:05:53.360
I'm not sure if he's having
a blood clot happening there.

00:05:53.360 --> 00:05:55.860
The swelling is very worrisome to me.

00:05:55.860 --> 00:05:59.070
That would be like an
urgent ultrasound, a duplex

00:05:59.070 --> 00:06:00.810
to see if that there is a clot forming.

00:06:00.810 --> 00:06:04.130
A lot of times when you
are sedated in a surgery,

00:06:04.130 --> 00:06:06.370
you can have a higher
chance of developing a DVT,

00:06:06.370 --> 00:06:08.490
which is known as a deep venous thrombosis

00:06:08.490 --> 00:06:10.500
where the blood being
stagnant in your veins,

00:06:10.500 --> 00:06:12.370
'cause you're not moving,
you're just laying down

00:06:12.370 --> 00:06:13.203
forms a clot.

00:06:13.203 --> 00:06:14.865
The danger of it, if it gets dislodged

00:06:14.865 --> 00:06:17.250
and it continues into the circulation,

00:06:17.250 --> 00:06:18.540
that could actually end up in your lungs,

00:06:18.540 --> 00:06:20.290
lock off circulation in your lungs

00:06:20.290 --> 00:06:21.870
causing a pulmonary embolism.

00:06:21.870 --> 00:06:24.080
- They said, "Ice the (beep) out of it."

00:06:24.080 --> 00:06:27.430
So all I did for a period
of five or six days

00:06:27.430 --> 00:06:29.810
was to sit on the couch and ice my knee.

00:06:29.810 --> 00:06:33.320
Wow, video games are so cool.

00:06:33.320 --> 00:06:36.290
- The earlier we can get
even some slight motion

00:06:36.290 --> 00:06:39.100
post surgically, in an
area that was operated on,

00:06:39.100 --> 00:06:40.120
the better outcomes we get.

00:06:40.120 --> 00:06:42.330
And I'm not talking about him
going and starting squatting.

00:06:42.330 --> 00:06:44.200
I'm talking about just gentle mobility.

00:06:44.200 --> 00:06:47.190
We actually even have
patients that have like robots

00:06:47.190 --> 00:06:48.950
and machines do the motion for them

00:06:48.950 --> 00:06:50.620
just to articulate the joint,

00:06:50.620 --> 00:06:53.090
just to get that fluid
coming back into that area.

00:06:53.090 --> 00:06:55.040
'Cause the longer we
leave things locked up,

00:06:55.040 --> 00:06:56.850
the harder it is to unlock them after.

00:06:56.850 --> 00:06:59.680
- [Ned] Oh my God, look at that.

00:06:59.680 --> 00:07:03.200
I have little tiny holes in my knee.

00:07:03.200 --> 00:07:05.600
- So that goes to show he
did have arthroscopic surgery

00:07:05.600 --> 00:07:08.520
because he had cameras inserted
to those holes and tools.

00:07:08.520 --> 00:07:10.230
And you see that marker there,

00:07:10.230 --> 00:07:11.520
which is weird that they
didn't take that off.

00:07:11.520 --> 00:07:13.290
That just shows that they were operating

00:07:13.290 --> 00:07:15.500
on that knee before
they started operating.

00:07:15.500 --> 00:07:16.348
That's how we know.

00:07:16.348 --> 00:07:17.181
- [Ned] You guys ready to see pictures

00:07:17.181 --> 00:07:18.310
of the inside of my knee?

00:07:18.310 --> 00:07:20.340
- Yeah, you bet ya.

00:07:20.340 --> 00:07:22.430
- [Ned] This is like the
inside of my knee cavity--

00:07:22.430 --> 00:07:23.728
- They have no idea what they're--

00:07:23.728 --> 00:07:24.880
- [Ned] These are like my aPLs and stuff.

00:07:24.880 --> 00:07:27.830
And this is a giant lump of scar tissue

00:07:27.830 --> 00:07:29.150
that they shaved away.

00:07:29.150 --> 00:07:30.840
- You could have told me
that these were photos

00:07:30.840 --> 00:07:32.070
of your unborn child and
I'd been like, oh my gosh,

00:07:32.070 --> 00:07:33.153
she's beautiful.

00:07:33.153 --> 00:07:33.986
(Ned laughs)

00:07:33.986 --> 00:07:36.130
- The doctor told me,
you know, it'll be a week

00:07:36.130 --> 00:07:38.710
of being off your feet and
then I'll be back to normal.

00:07:38.710 --> 00:07:40.300
I can help Ariel out 'cause you know,

00:07:40.300 --> 00:07:42.050
she's still pregnant and it'll be great.

00:07:42.050 --> 00:07:45.660
As the weeks go, on my knee
doesn't get a ton better.

00:07:45.660 --> 00:07:48.930
Today, I'm going to get
my sutures taken out.

00:07:48.930 --> 00:07:51.907
They kicked them in for
an extra couple of weeks.

00:07:51.907 --> 00:07:53.600
- Oh you see, there's the robot

00:07:53.600 --> 00:07:55.780
that I was talking about earlier
about getting mobility back

00:07:55.780 --> 00:07:56.613
into that knee.

00:07:56.613 --> 00:07:57.990
- Two weeks, three weeks, four weeks

00:07:57.990 --> 00:07:59.900
and still I couldn't play soccer.

00:07:59.900 --> 00:08:01.460
- That's not a surprise to me.

00:08:01.460 --> 00:08:03.810
Four weeks post-op,
you're not playing soccer.

00:08:03.810 --> 00:08:05.080
Like that's just the norm.

00:08:05.080 --> 00:08:06.150
I sprained my ankle.

00:08:06.150 --> 00:08:07.857
I don't expect to play
soccer in four weeks.

00:08:07.857 --> 00:08:08.690
- During this time,

00:08:08.690 --> 00:08:12.540
they gave me a prescription
to painkillers.

00:08:12.540 --> 00:08:16.970
So here I am exactly in the
place that I didn't want to be,

00:08:16.970 --> 00:08:19.840
doing all the things
that I didn't wanna do.

00:08:19.840 --> 00:08:22.550
I have a prescription full of painkillers.

00:08:22.550 --> 00:08:24.912
And I am saying to them, not today.

00:08:24.912 --> 00:08:28.290
(pills rattling)

00:08:28.290 --> 00:08:29.730
- That is not the responsible way

00:08:29.730 --> 00:08:31.240
of getting rid of painkillers.

00:08:31.240 --> 00:08:32.870
There's pharmacies that
will take them back.

00:08:32.870 --> 00:08:35.360
And also if you're ever throwing
away medications like that,

00:08:35.360 --> 00:08:38.310
rip your name off the label
just for privacy's sake

00:08:38.310 --> 00:08:39.280
or at least black it out

00:08:39.280 --> 00:08:40.790
just so no one else can get your info.

00:08:40.790 --> 00:08:42.518
- It's time for physical therapy.

00:08:42.518 --> 00:08:44.060
(upbeat music)

00:08:44.060 --> 00:08:46.240
- Look at this guy, resistance bands...

00:08:46.240 --> 00:08:47.434
- [Ned] Not wearing many clothing.

00:08:47.434 --> 00:08:48.840
You know, you could just see everything

00:08:48.840 --> 00:08:49.890
move a little better.

00:08:49.890 --> 00:08:50.950
I kept at it.

00:08:50.950 --> 00:08:52.380
Luckily Ariel was there.

00:08:52.380 --> 00:08:55.460
Yeah I mean, I honestly couldn't
have done it without Ariel.

00:08:55.460 --> 00:08:58.200
I had the support of a
great physical therapist,

00:08:58.200 --> 00:09:01.880
kept working, kept icing,
it slowly got better.

00:09:01.880 --> 00:09:05.930
Finally, several months
after the recovery process,

00:09:05.930 --> 00:09:09.850
when Wes was growing up
before our very eyes,

00:09:09.850 --> 00:09:11.870
I got to play my first soccer game.

00:09:11.870 --> 00:09:13.720
- Very reasonable timeframe.

00:09:13.720 --> 00:09:15.420
Like honestly, when I talk to patients

00:09:15.420 --> 00:09:16.790
that they're getting major knee surgery,

00:09:16.790 --> 00:09:19.380
I would say six to 12 months, no soccer.

00:09:19.380 --> 00:09:21.971
So seven months is totally reasonable.

00:09:21.971 --> 00:09:25.240
- I was able to run
full speed on the field,

00:09:25.240 --> 00:09:27.440
cut back and forth, kick the ball.

00:09:27.440 --> 00:09:30.150
Yes, sure my knee buckled a couple times.

00:09:30.150 --> 00:09:31.660
Yes, sure I wasn't perfect.

00:09:31.660 --> 00:09:35.010
But, I was back to doing the
things I enjoyed the most.

00:09:35.010 --> 00:09:37.930
- Markiplier also has made
videos about his medical journey.

00:09:37.930 --> 00:09:39.750
Click here to check
out my reaction to that

00:09:39.750 --> 00:09:41.680
and as always stay happy and healthy.

00:09:41.680 --> 00:09:43.265
I'll see you at Markiplier's video.

00:09:43.265 --> 00:09:45.932
(upbeat music)

